  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1232 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nToday through Thursday: Increasing Rain Chances on Thursday\n\nAn H500 low pressure system is currently located over the Desert \nSouthwest but will eventually lift northeast towards the Central \nPlains. Before that occurs, another very nice Fall day is in store \nfor the Driftless Region. Partly to mostly cloudy skies are expected \nalong with afternoon highs in the low to mid 60s.\n\nThe aforementioned low pressure system is forecast to merge with a \nupper level trough that will dig south out of the Canadian Prairie \nProvinces on Thursday. Moisture will surge northward out ahead of \nthese features which will aid in the development of rain showers \nbeginning on Thursday morning. Widespread rainfall is becoming more \nand more likely and precipitation chances continue to increase \nupwards of 80-100%. The potential for a half inch of rainfall or \nmore continues to slowly increase and the new NBM has the potential \nfor a half inch or more at 40-60% area-wide. The high end rainfall \namounts (5-10% chance at occurrence) are only upwards of 1.5-2 \ninches, so impacts from this system should be minimal. Some \nlingering light rainfall may remain over the area through the night, \nbut drier, cooler air moving in behind a cold front should put an \nend to most of the rain showers by late Thursday evening.\n\nFriday into Saturday: Strong Winds Friday - Frost/Freeze Saturday \nMorning\n\nBreezy to windy conditions are likely to start the day on Friday in \nthe wake of the cold frontal passage. The surface pressure gradient \nwill tighten behind the front and a LLJ is also expected to set up \nwith H850 winds upwards of 40-50 kts. Widespread wind gusts \nassociated with the strong northwest wind are forecast to be 30-40 \nmph with a low to medium chance (20-40%) to exceed 45 mph. Even \nthough it will be at least breezy all day, the strongest wind is \nexpected during the morning into early afternoon hours. The cooler \nairmass behind the front should aid in dropping temperatures down \nwell into the 30s and perhaps even the upper 20s on Friday night \ninto Saturday morning. Just how low temperatures may drop will be \nlargely influenced by how light the surface wind will be. The \npotential for at least frost conditions is very high (80-100%) but \nthe hard freeze conditions (28 degrees or below) may only occur \nalong and north of I-94. The 13/01z NBM has a 30-50% chance to drop \ndown to 28 degrees or less along and north of the I-94 corridor. \nSoutherly wind on Saturday should bring in some warmer air and \nafternoon highs may return to the lower 60s.\n\nSunday into Next Week: Dry and Warm Conditions Expected\n\nUpper level high pressure quickly builds back in behind the \ndeparting trough on Sunday. High temperatures should quickly rebound \ninto the 60s to lower 70s during this time frame. Dry conditions are \nexpected at this time even though there are a few outlier solutions \nthat produce light precipitation across the Midwest early next week. \nConfidence in that scenario panning out is very low even though \nthere appears to be a plume of mid level moisture that will be in \nplace over the region.",
